@page "/"

<PageTitle>SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML - AI in C#, on your GPU, in your browser</PageTitle>

<div class="hero-section">
    <div class="hero-content">
        <h1 class="hero-title"><span class="gradient-text">SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML</span></h1>
        <p class="hero-subtitle">Neural network inference and training in C#, on any GPU, in the browser or on the desktop</p>
        <p class="hero-description">
            Every layer, from a matrix multiply to a 5.6B-parameter image model, is a C# kernel that
            <a href="https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU" target="_blank">SpawnDev.ILGPU</a> compiles for the device in front of it.
            No ONNX Runtime, no native binaries, no server: the demos below run on <em>your</em> GPU, and nothing you type, draw or upload leaves this tab.
        </p>
        <figure class="hero-media">
            <img src="screenshots/2026-10-20_TextTo3D-Rig-Animate-Pirate-FLUX3-Qwen3.gif" alt="A text prompt becomes a rigged, animated 3D pirate, entirely in the browser" width="512" height="512" />
            <figcaption>One prompt, one browser tab: Qwen3 writes the caption, FLUX 3 draws it, TRELLIS.2 builds the textured mesh, SkinTokens rigs it, Kimodo animates it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="hero-buttons">
            <a href="text-to-3d" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Try Text to 3D</a>
            <a href="getting-started" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">Use it in your app</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML" target="_blank" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">GitHub</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="features-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Start here</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">The flagship demos. Models download once from models.spawndev.com and stay cached in your browser. A desktop GPU with WebGPU (Chrome or Edge) is recommended for the generative ones.</p>
    <div class="demo-grid">
        @foreach (var d in Featured)
        {
            <a class="demo-card" href="@d.Route">
                <div class="demo-card-head">
                    <h3>@d.Title</h3>
                    <span class="status @(d.Verified ? "status-verified" : "status-beta")">@(d.Verified ? "Verified" : "Beta")</span>
                </div>
                <p>@d.Text</p>
                <div class="demo-models">@d.Models</div>
            </a>
        }
    </div>

    <h3 class="subsection-title">More demos</h3>
    <div class="chip-row">
        @foreach (var (route, label) in MoreDemos)
        {
            <a href="@route" class="try-link">@label</a>
        }
    </div>
    <p class="section-desc small">What is verified, partial or still in progress, with the test behind each claim: <a href="https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML/blob/master/Docs/DEMO_AND_MODEL_STATUS.md" target="_blank">DEMO_AND_MODEL_STATUS.md</a>.</p>
</div>

<div class="features-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What it can do</h2>
    <div class="features-grid">
        <div class="feature-card">
            <h3>Images, video and 3D</h3>
            <p>FLUX 3 Image Klein (text to image, edits from up to 10 reference pictures), FLUX.2 klein, Stable Diffusion 1.x / 2.x / SDXL with LoRA and ControlNet, LTX-Video and Wan 2.2 video, TRELLIS.2 text and image to textured 3D, SkinTokens rigging and Kimodo animation. Each ported stage is checked against its reference implementation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
            <h3>Language models</h3>
            <p>Quantized GGUF LLMs (Llama, Qwen, Gemma, Mistral, Phi and more) with KV-cache decode replayed from a recorded command plan, TurboQuant cache compression, and batched decode of many answers in one pass over the weights.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
            <h3>Training</h3>
            <p>LoRA fine-tuning of GGUF language models on the device: llama3.1:8b on a 12 GB card, 1.78 s per 1,600-token step on an RTX 4070 (4.53 s on an RTX 2060), PEFT adapter files, a step checked against PyTorch. Small networks train right here in the browser: <a href="train">Draw to Learn</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
            <h3>Speech</h3>
            <p>Whisper speech-to-text (the same transcript on all six backends), Kokoro text-to-speech, ZipVoice voice cloning from a short clip, voice-activity and smart-turn detection.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
            <h3>Seven model formats</h3>
            <p>ONNX, TFLite, GGUF, SafeTensors, TensorFlow GraphDef, PyTorch checkpoints and Core ML, detected from their bytes and lowered to one graph. @OperatorCount ONNX operators, a graph optimizer and fused kernels for attention, normalization and quantized matmul.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
            <h3>Zero-copy, trim and AOT safe</h3>
            <p>Pixels, activations and weights stay on the GPU. In the browser, weights stream from <code>fetch</code> and OPFS straight into GPU buffers without touching the .NET heap. The library is trimmable and runs under Blazor WebAssembly AOT.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="features-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Measured</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">RTX 4070 12 GB, models cached. Methods and more numbers are in the <a href="https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML/tree/master/Docs" target="_blank">docs</a>.</p>
    <div class="perf-table-wrap">
        <table class="perf-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Where</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Photo to textured 3D mesh (TRELLIS.2)</td><td>Chrome, WebGPU</td><td>134 to 148 s</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Photo to textured 3D mesh</td><td>CUDA</td><td>about 40 s</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Animate a picture, 704 x 480, 97 frames (LTX-Video)</td><td>Chrome, WebGPU</td><td>59 s (30 s with the model loaded)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FLUX 3 Image Klein, 512 px, 16 steps</td><td>CUDA</td><td>26.1 s (4-bit)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Prompt to rigged, animated character</td><td>CUDA</td><td>77 s</td></tr>
                <tr><td>LoRA training step, llama3.1:8b, 1,600 tokens</td><td>CUDA</td><td>1.78 s</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="features-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">How it works</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
        Kernels are written in C# against <a href="https://github.com/m4rs-mt/ILGPU" target="_blank">ILGPU</a>.
        At runtime SpawnDev.ILGPU compiles each one to the shader language or binary of the backend you picked.
    </p>
    <div class="pipeline-visual">
        <div class="pipeline-box pipeline-source">Model file</div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow"></div>
        <div class="pipeline-box pipeline-compiler">Format parser</div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow"></div>
        <div class="pipeline-box pipeline-compiler">Graph optimizer</div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow"></div>
        <div class="pipeline-box pipeline-compiler">C# kernels</div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow"></div>
        <div class="pipeline-targets">
            <span class="pipeline-target target-browser">WGSL</span>
            <span class="pipeline-target target-browser">GLSL</span>
            <span class="pipeline-target target-browser">Wasm</span>
            <span class="pipeline-target target-desktop">PTX</span>
            <span class="pipeline-target target-desktop">OpenCL C</span>
            <span class="pipeline-target target-desktop">SIMD CPU</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="subsection-title">Browser (Blazor WebAssembly)</h3>
    <div class="features-grid features-grid-3">
        <div class="feature-card feature-highlight-browser">
            <h3>WebGPU</h3>
            <p>WGSL compute shaders with shared memory, barriers and atomics. Runs every demo on this site, including the generative models.</p>
            <div class="feature-tag">GPU, fastest in the browser</div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card feature-highlight-browser">
            <h3>WebGL</h3>
            <p>GLSL ES 3.0 through transform feedback. Works on almost every browser and device, including older laptops and phones.</p>
            <div class="feature-tag">GPU, widest reach</div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card feature-highlight-browser">
            <h3>Wasm</h3>
            <p>WebAssembly kernels dispatched to Web Workers, with shared memory and barriers. Runs where no GPU API is available.</p>
            <div class="feature-tag">CPU, universal fallback</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="subsection-title">Desktop (console, WPF, ASP.NET)</h3>
    <div class="features-grid features-grid-3">
        <div class="feature-card feature-highlight-desktop">
            <h3>CUDA</h3>
            <p>PTX for NVIDIA GPUs, with tensor-core GEMMs (mma, ldmatrix, cp.async) from Turing on, and CUDA graphs for decode.</p>
            <div class="feature-tag">NVIDIA GPU</div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card feature-highlight-desktop">
            <h3>OpenCL</h3>
            <p>Cross-vendor GPU compute on NVIDIA, AMD and Intel GPUs and integrated graphics.</p>
            <div class="feature-tag">Any GPU</div>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card feature-highlight-desktop">
            <h3>CPU</h3>
            <p>The Velocity SIMD backend by default on 64-bit desktops, plus the exact interpreting CPU accelerator the tests compare against.</p>
            <div class="feature-tag">SIMD CPU, reference</div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

